00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:44.870
What am I doing now? So this is the uh

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.190
pre-release phase let's call it for

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.830
Puffer 5.0. So, we have a big release

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:52.710
coming out soon, right? And I'm pulling

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:56.710
in now like 15ish new environments

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.110
written by contributors. That's a lot of

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:03.110
code to triage and a lot of it is uh

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.190
targeted at an old version of Puffer and

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.150
needs to be upgraded to our latest API

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.430
and a lot of it has like subtle bugs.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:14.230
So, I'm essentially semi-automatically

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:16.070
going through and having Grock look for

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.630
all the patterns I know for like common

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:21.190
errors that people make. Um, I'm going

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.789
through and looking for common patterns

00:18:22.799 --> 00:18:25.350
that I know LLM screw up and making sure

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:27.110
that the LLM hasn't messed up all the

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.070
nice code. And I also have this this

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:33.029
thing has access to a 8GPU box. So, I'm

00:18:33.039 --> 00:18:34.630
also having this thing orchestrate some

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:36.070
test runs to make sure that everything

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:38.150
is training correctly and building new

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.870
binaries for our website. So whole bunch

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:43.590
of stuff. Um, but this is considered for

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.470
me this is like generally pretty brain

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:50.549
dead easy routine work. Um, the stuff

00:18:50.559 --> 00:18:52.950
that's a lot more interesting is like

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:56.070
the detailed research work that I do or

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:58.310
the heavy performance engineering that I

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:00.789
do for the core of Puffer.

00:19:00.799 --> 00:19:02.470
Those tend to be the more interesting

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:05.750
sessions. This weekend, since I have the

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.830
weekend to myself and we're very close

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.150
to release, I'm kind of just marathon

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.310
streaming until I have the entire

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:14.390
pre-release done, which is why I'm

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.430
pulling a lot of hours.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:19.029
I think we can get it all done this

00:19:19.039 --> 00:19:20.710
weekend, though. I think we can get it

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:43.909
all done.

00:20:07.909 --> 00:20:07.919
When did I start coding? What age? I

00:20:07.919 --> 00:20:10.630
like 11 or 12.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:13.029
Maybe 13 or 14. Actually making some

00:20:13.039 --> 00:20:17.110
real things. And then probably like 16

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:18.789
before anything that I wrote wasn't

00:20:18.799 --> 00:20:25.830
complete and utter crap.

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:28.320
Long-term vision for Puffer. We are

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.149
going to make reinforcement learning

00:20:30.159 --> 00:20:32.710
sane, stable, and consistent. It is

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:36.149
going to be a mature area of AI that

00:20:36.159 --> 00:20:38.149
just works out of the box on a huge

00:20:38.159 --> 00:20:40.310
range of problems and is so fast that

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.230
you can train models for specific tasks

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.430
at home on a single GPU.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.270
We're actually most of the way there.

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.110
Like I think we're like probably 75% of

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.950
the way there actually. I've been

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.110
working on this for three years. I think

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:56.710
basically we have it within the next

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:01.190
year.

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:04.720
I can show the codebase real quick.

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:07.110
Check this out. So this is the highest

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.470
performance reinforcement learning

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.549
codebase in existence by about three

00:21:10.559 --> 00:21:13.510
orders of magnitude

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.590
as of well ignore this. This is this is

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:18.390
getting deleted but and this is also 400

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.149
lines of LLM bloat that I have to

00:21:20.159 --> 00:21:24.470
delete. Stupid LLMs. Um, so this is only

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.510
10k lines. Like the entire core I it

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:29.110
took a long time to get it to be this

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:32.149
short. It's only 10k lines. The core

00:21:32.159 --> 00:21:34.630
training and everything, no pietorch,

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:37.190
everything from scratch in cuda c is

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.549
only 5k lines. It's this puffl file and

00:21:40.559 --> 00:21:43.190
then this algo file. And then we have

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.510
our own visualization toolkit which is

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:48.630
one file,600 lines. We have our own CPU

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.149
eval infrastructure. This is the thing

00:21:50.159 --> 00:21:52.390
the LLM loaded. It should only be about

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:54.390
a thousand lines. And then we have our

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:57.350
own envi. And we have uh protein, which

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:59.590
is our hyperp sweeps. Pretty much

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:04.230
everything.

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:07.520
How many hours do I code per week? Like

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.310
I don't know, anywhere from 20 to 90.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:51.990
[laughter] It really depends, man.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:53.280
But yeah, I mean, if you're looking to

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:55.110
learn, we've literally had high school

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:56.549
students contribute environments to

00:22:56.559 --> 00:22:58.789
Puffer. Obviously, the high end of all

00:22:58.799 --> 00:23:00.789
the research stuff that we do takes

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:03.029
quite a bit of experience to get into,

00:23:03.039 --> 00:23:05.510
but honestly, like programming

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:07.029
reinforcement learning environments is

00:23:07.039 --> 00:23:08.789
kind of just like doing low-level game

00:23:08.799 --> 00:23:11.350
development. Um, you can make something

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:12.870
meaningful in only a couple hundred

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:15.430
lines of code and you can train it at

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:16.390
home.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:17.830
good way to learn some low-level dev.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:19.190
It's a good way to get some exposure to

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.270
RL. And some of the environments even

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.710
end up being directly useful in

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:25.029
research. Believe it or not, it's

00:23:25.039 --> 00:23:26.549
actually pretty hard to anticipate

00:23:26.559 --> 00:23:29.029
upfront the uh the types of environments

00:23:29.039 --> 00:23:31.909
that are going to be very helpful.

00:23:31.919 --> 00:23:34.870
Getting into Fang,

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:38.549
I uh I think you might end up being a

00:23:38.559 --> 00:23:41.909
little bit disappointed. Um,

